        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "ParserResource.doGet() is a JAX-RS endpoint annotated @Produces(\"text/html\") that streams writeFlickerFeed()'s output directly to the browser. That method builds the entire HTML document (\"<html><body>...\" and an <img src=\"...\"> tag) by raw string concatenation of 'id', 'farm', 'server', 'secret' fields parsed straight out of a remote JSON feed, with zero HTML-escaping/encoding call anywhere in the method or available in the codebase (no HTML escape/encode/sanitize utility exists in this repo at all -- only JSON-string escaping (writeEscapedString), URL-encoding (Twitter demo), and JSON-pointer encoding were found). If the upstream Flickr response is ever attacker-influenced or MITM'd, those fields are reflected into the HTML response completely unescaped -- the standard output-escaping control is absent.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Source inspection confirms the claim precisely. ParserResource.doGet() (line 42-50) is a JAX-RS @GET @Produces(\"text/html\") handler registered in DemoApplication's resource set, so it is a live REST endpoint. It returns a StreamingOutput whose write() calls writeFlickerFeed(os) (line 47), which fetches a remote JSON feed over plain HTTP from api.flickr.com (line 53, not even TLS, making MITM substitution of the response plausible) and parses it with a streaming JsonParser. The 'id', 'server', 'secret' and 'farm' string fields are read directly via parser.getString() (lines 65-82) and concatenated with zero encoding into raw HTML via ps.println(\"<html><body>\") (line 61) and ps.println(\"<img src=\\\"http://farm\"+farm+\".staticflickr.com/\"+server+\"/\"+id+\"_\"+secret+\".jpg\\\">\") (line 74), followed by ps.println(\"</body></html>\") (line 87). No escaping/sanitizing call appears anywhere in the method or call chain. A graph-wide search for escape/encode/sanitize calls across the whole repo (CpgCall.name CONTAINS 'escape'/'encode'/'sanitiz') returns only JsonPatchImpl.encodePointer, JsonTokenizer.unescape, and TwitterObjectSearch's URL-encode calls \u2014 no HTML-escaping utility exists anywhere in the codebase, matching the analyst's claim. Reachability is also confirmed in-graph: every CpgCall in writeFlickerFeed (including the println at line 61 and the unescaped concatenation+println at line 74) has reachable_from_entry=true, and two EntryPoint nodes (kind='handler', exposure='reachable') point at ParserResource.doGet and its StreamingOutput.write lambda, confirming this is a live, reachable sink from an exposed entry point, not a dead code path."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
