            "decision": "CONFIRM",
            "sinkCentrality": 0.0,
            "verifyRoute": "full",
            "severity": "MEDIUM",
            "security-severity": "5.5"
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-319",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "In the same ParserResource.writeFlickerFeed(), the outbound request to Flickr's API is made over plain HTTP (http://api.flickr.com/...) rather than HTTPS, and it carries a hardcoded API key (api_key=221160312e1c22ec60ecf336951b0e77) directly in the URL query string in cleartext. There is no transport-encryption control (no https:// anywhere in this request) protecting the credential in transit, so anyone on the network path (including, notably, an attacker who could also tamper with the unencrypted response body that is then reflected unescaped into HTML -- see companion finding) can observe/capture the key.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Read the actual source and confirmed the lead exactly as described. ParserResource.writeFlickerFeed() builds `new URL(\"http://api.flickr.com/services/rest/?method=flickr.photos.getRecent&api_key=221160312e1c22ec60ecf336951b0e77&format=json&nojsoncallback=1&per_page=20\")` (line 53) and immediately calls `url.openStream()` (line 54) \u2014 a plain-HTTP outbound request carrying a hardcoded Flickr API key in the cleartext query string, with no TLS anywhere on this call path. Graph confirms reachability: writeFlickerFeed is called from doGet() (reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=2), and doGet is the write() body of a StreamingOutput returned by a JAX-RS @GET endpoint registered as EntryPoint (exposure='reachable', kind='handler') \u2014 so this is attacker-reachable simply by hitting the /parser endpoint, no extra conditions needed. Cross-checked the analyst's comparator claim: grepped all demos/ for https?:// \u2014 demos/facebook/.../FacebookObjectSearch.java:67 uses `https://graph.facebook.com/...` and demos/twitter/.../TwitterObjectSearch.java:69 uses `https://api.twitter.com/...`, while this Flickr call is the only outbound http:// (non-TLS) API call with an embedded credential in the whole codebase. This matches CWE-319 (Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information) for the API key in transit. Severity kept MEDIUM rather than HIGH: this is sample/demo code (org.eclipse.parsson.demos.rest), the key is already publicly committed in source regardless of transport, and Flickr API keys are low-privilege, rate-limited, non-account-compromising credentials \u2014 so the added risk from the HTTP transport specifically is real but bounded."
          },
          "locations": [
