        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-294",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "TwitterObjectSearch.getSearchStream() builds the OAuth 1.0 request-signing parameter map with a hardcoded, constant 'oauth_nonce' value (\"4b25256957d75b6370f33a4501dc5e7e\") instead of generating a fresh random/unique nonce per request (there is a '// TODO' comment directly above it, and no SecureRandom/UUID-based nonce generation exists anywhere in the codebase). OAuth 1.0's anti-replay protection depends on each request using a unique nonce; a captured request (e.g. via logs, a proxy, or the same timestamp window) could be replayed against Twitter's API using this code's signature. TwitterStreamSearch.main() reuses this same getSearchStream() method, so the gap is shared by both Twitter demos.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly against the real source. In TwitterObjectSearch.getSearchStream() the OAuth 1.0 parameter map is built with a literal, hardcoded oauth_nonce value immediately preceded by a '// TODO' marker, rather than any random/unique value: `put(\"oauth_nonce\", \"4b25256957d75b6370f33a4501dc5e7e\"); // TODO` (line 84). A repo-wide grep for `SecureRandom|UUID\\.randomUUID|oauth_nonce` across the tree returns only this single line \u2014 confirming no nonce-generation logic exists anywhere in the codebase; the same constant is used on every invocation. TwitterStreamSearch.main() (line 44) calls `TwitterObjectSearch.getSearchStream()` directly, so it reuses the identical hardcoded nonce \u2014 both demo entry points are affected. Graph query confirms both `TwitterObjectSearch.main` and `TwitterStreamSearch.main` are EntryPoints with exposure='reachable' and `reachable_from_entry=true`, and the TreeMap-construction calls at that site are also `reachable_from_entry=true` (hop_distance=3), so the flawed code executes on every normal run of either demo, not merely on an edge-case path. Since OAuth 1.0's anti-replay defense depends on each signed request carrying a unique nonce (combined with the timestamp) that the server rejects on reuse, a hardcoded nonce means any request captured (via logs, a MITM proxy, etc.) within the same timestamp-validity window could be replayed to Twitter's API with a valid signature, authenticating as the victim. This is a genuine, present-in-source logic flaw, not a graph artifact \u2014 I confirmed it by reading the file content myself, independent of the analyst's claim."
          },
