        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-862",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The demo JAX-RS POST endpoints (ArrayResource.doPost, ObjectResource.doPost, StructureResource.doPost) accept arbitrary JSON structures from any network caller with no authentication or authorization check anywhere on the request path -- no @RolesAllowed/@PermitAll/session/login-check call appears anywhere in the demos/rest module. Current impact is low (the handlers only System.out.println the parsed structure), but the standard access-control control (authn/authz on a state-accepting route) is entirely absent, so if this sample were extended to do anything consequential with the POST body it would be wide open.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly against source and graph. All three JAX-RS POST handlers (ArrayResource.doPost, ObjectResource.doPost, StructureResource.doPost) are registered via DemoApplication.getClasses() with no ContainerRequestFilter/Interceptor, no @RolesAllowed/@PermitAll/@DenyAll annotation, and no web.xml security-constraint anywhere in the demos/rest module. Each handler body consists solely of `System.out.println(structure)` -- no auth check precedes it. Cypher query over the whole scan confirms the EntryPoint nodes for all three doPost methods have exposure='reachable', kind='handler', reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0 (i.e. they are directly-reachable, unauthenticated entry points). A second cypher query for any call containing 'login'/'authenticate'/'Authorization'/'RolesAllowed'/'session' across the entire codebase returns exactly one hit, an outbound Authorization header set by the unrelated Twitter demo client (demos/twitter/.../TwitterObjectSearch.java:146) -- confirming zero authorization logic exists anywhere near these REST endpoints. This matches the analyst's claim precisely, and the lead's own reachable_from_entry=true signal (not false) further supports exploitability rather than undercutting it."
          },
