        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-330",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "In the Twitter demo's OAuth1 request-signing code, the oauth_nonce value is hardcoded to the constant string \"4b25256957d75b6370f33a4501dc5e7e\" instead of being randomly generated per request, with a bare '// TODO' comment sitting directly above the hardcoded value -- indicating the developer flagged this as incomplete/wrong and never fixed it. A nonce is supposed to be unique per OAuth request to prevent replay attacks; reusing the same nonce for every request defeats that protection. The enclosing method getSearchStream() is reachable from entry (main -> getSearchStream, hop_distance 2) per the precomputed reachability, i.e. it executes on every run of this demo.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly against the real source. TwitterObjectSearch.java line 84 hardcodes the OAuth1 oauth_nonce to the literal string \"4b25256957d75b6370f33a4501dc5e7e\" inside the TreeMap initializer built in getSearchStream() (lines 81-90), with a \"// TODO\" marker on that same line indicating the developer flagged it as unfinished. Since oauth_nonce never varies between invocations, every signed request this demo sends carries the same nonce (only oauth_timestamp changes), weakening/defeating the replay-protection purpose of the nonce \u2014 a textbook CWE-330 (use of insufficiently random/predictable value in a security context). Graph queries confirm the method chain is live on normal program flow: main (line 46, hop_distance=0, reachable_from_entry=true) -> getSearchStream (line 67, hop_distance=2, reachable_from_entry=true) -> the TreeMap call-site at line 81 (hop_distance=3, reachable_from_entry=true). One correction to the analyst's description: in the actual file the \"// TODO\" is a trailing comment on the SAME line as the put(\"oauth_nonce\", ...) call (line 84), not a separate line directly above it \u2014 the graph's serialized/reformatted code dump displays it on its own line, which is a cosmetic CPG normalization artifact, not a different code structure. This does not change the substance of the finding."
          },
